course: everyday
lesson: 413
title: Loot boxes and the surprise trap
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Kai wanted the golden helmet. It had a one-in-a-hundred chance of dropping from a loot box, a box you pay for without knowing what is inside. He opened one: a green scarf. Another: a blue scarf. Ten boxes later Kai owned a rainbow of scarves, no helmet, and no gems at all. Every time a box shook and glowed before opening, his heart jumped — and that jump, his dad said, is exactly what the box is built to sell.
explain: Leo's dad called loot boxes «a fruit machine for kids». Leo thought that was a bit much, so his dad explained: a fruit machine takes your money, spins, flashes lights, and now and then pays out, so you keep feeding it. Leo thought about the spinning, glowing, «almost!» box and went quiet. «Huh,» he said. Theo had the opposite problem: he got a rare item in his very first box and decided he was lucky. The next twenty boxes gave him nothing special. One early win had made him keep paying for a long time.
explain: Mrs Okafor did the maths with the class. «A one percent chance means one in a hundred. Does that mean you win by box one hundred?» Most hands went up for yes. Then she showed them: each box is a fresh try that does not remember the last one, so even after a hundred boxes, about one person in three still has not won. The class groaned so loudly the next classroom knocked on the wall. Priya had already learned a similar thing: the YouTuber who opened a hundred boxes in a video had been given them free by the company — the luck on screen was an advert.
explain: The cast found their own ways out. Ruby saw a «LAST CHANCE: 2 hours left!» timer on a box, waited, and the same box came back the next week. Ollie and Jayden made a pact — no loot boxes this term — and saved enough to buy a whole new game together. Zara noticed she was logging in every day only for the free box, not for fun, and stopped. Kai's mum switched off in-game spending in the console settings; Kai was furious for a day, then found the game was just as fun without the shop. Noah's uncle showed him an old game with no shop at all, and they played it until dinner went cold.
fact: A loot box is a paid surprise: you pay before you know what you will get.
fact: Rare items in loot boxes are made rare on purpose, so that people keep buying.
fact: A one percent chance means about one in a hundred, on average, not one in every hundred tries for sure.
fact: Each loot box is a new try; the game does not «owe» you a win because you lost before.
fact: With a one percent chance, about one person in three still has nothing rare after a hundred boxes.
fact: Paying money for a random chance at a prize works like gambling, and some countries, such as Belgium, have treated paid loot boxes as gambling.
fact: Lights, shaking and near-misses make opening a box feel exciting, so you want to try again.
fact: An early lucky win can make people keep paying for a long time, hoping it will happen again.
fact: Countdown timers on offers are there to rush you, so you buy before you think.
fact: Videos of people opening many loot boxes are sometimes paid for or supplied by the game company.
fact: A pact with a friend helps, because saying no is easier with someone beside you.
fact: Parents can switch off or lock in-game spending in the settings of most consoles, tablets and phones.
fact: Age ratings such as PEGI and ESRB add a note when a game has in-game purchases with random items.
exercise: What is a loot box? | A paid surprise where you do not know what you will get | A box that always holds the rarest item in the whole game | A free gift the game gives to everyone | A box for keeping your game discs tidy
exercise: How did Kai feel each time a box shook and glowed? | Excited, his heart jumped | Calm and bored | Angry at the box | Sleepy and fed up
exercise: What did Kai get after ten boxes? | A pile of scarves, no helmet and no gems left | The golden helmet on the tenth box, just in time | Ten golden helmets | His gems back with a bonus for trying
exercise: Why did Leo's dad call loot boxes «a fruit machine for kids»? | Both take money for a random chance at a prize | Both are shaped like a piece of fruit | Both are only allowed for children under twelve | Both always pay back more than you put in
exercise: Why did Theo keep buying after his first box? | An early lucky win made him think he was lucky | The game forced him to keep buying more and more | His friends paid for the boxes | He knew the next box would be rare
exercise: What happened in Theo's next twenty boxes? | Nothing special at all | A rare item in every single box | His money came back to him | The game gave him a helmet for free
exercise: A box has a one percent chance. What does that mean? | About one in a hundred on average | One win in every hundred boxes, for sure | One win in every ten boxes you buy | A win on the hundredth box exactly
exercise: Why did Mrs Okafor's class groan? | Even after a hundred boxes many still would not win | The maths lesson went on far too long, into break time | Nobody was allowed loot boxes in school | The boxes in the lesson were all empty
exercise: Why did the YouTuber look so lucky? | The company gave him the boxes for free | He knew a secret trick to open them | He opened them at the right time of day, just after midnight | He is naturally luckier than others
exercise: What happened after Ruby waited out the «last chance» timer? | The same box came back the next week | The box disappeared for ever and never came back | The game banned her for waiting | The box became free for her
exercise: What did Ollie and Jayden's pact give them? | Enough savings for a whole new game | A rare helmet each | A detention for making pacts in class | Nothing, they broke it on day one
exercise: Why did Zara stop logging in every day? | She noticed she was logging in for the box, not for fun | The game stopped giving out free boxes to anyone at all that month | Her console broke | Her friends told her to stop
exercise: How did Kai feel when his mum switched off spending? | Furious for a day, then fine | Happy and grateful straight away | Scared he would lose his account | Bored of the whole game for ever
exercise: What did Noah find in his uncle's old game? | It had no shop at all and was great fun | It sold even more loot boxes than the new games | It needed a password from Noah | It only worked with real coins
exercise: What is a wise thing to do before buying a loot box? | Do the maths on the chances first | Buy quickly while the box is still glowing | Buy more after a loss to win it back | Buy one for every friend
exercise: Which is another kind of courage? | Making a no-loot-box pact and keeping it | Buying boxes until you win | Telling everyone you got the helmet when you didn't | Spending your friend's gems
exercise: Kai has lost ten boxes in a row. What is true about box eleven? | Its chance is the same as the first box | It is almost sure to be the winner this time | It is less likely to win than the first | The game now owes him the helmet
test: «I've lost like 30 times, next one's GOTTA be it». right? | No, each box is a new try with the same chance | Yes, the game owes you a win by now | Yes, after 30 the chance doubles for every box | Only if the box glows gold
test: Leo: «fruit machine for kids? that's well dramatic, dad». What's the point his dad made? | Paying for a random chance at a prize is like gambling | Fruit machines are fine for kids, so loot boxes are too | Loot boxes are full of real fruit | Only grown-ups can open loot boxes
test: big lad at school: «I got a legendary first go, I'm lucky me». What's wise to tell him? | One lucky win does not mean the next will come | He should keep buying fast while his luck is still hot | Lucky people always win twice | He should buy boxes for everyone
test: the game flashes «LAST CHANCE 01:59:59». What's it for? | To rush you so you don't stop and think | To help you plan your money calmly and slowly | To show how long the whole game will last | To warn you the game will close for good
test: Priya: «this YouTuber opened 100 boxes, so jealous». What might be true? | The company may have given him the boxes | He paid for them all himself, for sure | He is the luckiest person alive | Loot boxes are free on YouTube
test: Ollie: «pact, no boxes this term, shake on it?» Why might it work? | Saying no is easier with someone beside you | Pacts make loot boxes cheaper for both of you | The game rewards pacts with gems | Jayden will pay if Ollie loses
test: Zara: «free box every day, can't miss it!!» What might the free box be for? | A hook to keep her coming back | A reward the game will lose money on | A thank-you with nothing behind it | A sign she is the best player in the game
test: Mrs Okafor: «100 people each open 100 boxes at one percent. Roughly how many get nothing rare?» | About a third of them | None of them | All of them | Exactly one of them
test: Kai, raging: «mum turned off the shop, the game's ruined!» A week later? | He found the game just as fun without the shop | He stopped playing for ever | The shop turned itself back on | He bought a new console
test: a game box says «In-game purchases (includes random items)». What does it tell you? | The game sells paid surprises like loot boxes | The game is free for ever, with nothing to pay | The game has no shop | The game is for under-sevens only
test: «box shook, glowed, I was SO close, nearly got it». What is the shaking and glowing for? | To make you excited so you try again | To show the box is about to break | To tell you the next one wins | To make the game run faster
exam: Identify the principal reason for which paid loot boxes have been likened to gambling. | Money is exchanged for a random chance of obtaining a prize | Loot boxes are lawful only for persons of full age | Loot boxes invariably return more value than is paid for them | Loot boxes are sold exclusively in establishments for betting
exam: Given that the probability of obtaining a rare item from a single box is one per cent, select the statement consistent with the established facts concerning one hundred successive boxes. | A rare item is not guaranteed, and roughly one purchaser in three obtains none | A rare item is guaranteed upon the hundredth box, though not before it | A rare item is guaranteed within the first ten boxes | The probability increases with each unsuccessful purchase until a rare item appears
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of a countdown timer displayed upon an offer? | It is intended to hasten a decision and discourage reflection | It is intended to assist the purchaser in budgeting | It is intended to signify the permanent withdrawal of the offer | It is intended to measure the duration of play
exam: Whereas a minor has experienced repeated temptation to purchase loot boxes, select the response most consistent with established guidance. | A parent ought to be asked to disable in-game spending in the device settings | The minor ought to purchase further boxes until the losses have been recovered | The minor ought to conceal all purchases from the household | The minor ought to borrow funds from acquaintances
